    hpi: "Vincent Langat, a 70-year-old Mechanic from Migori, was brought to casualty after a routine clinic blood test showed potassium of 7.0 mEq/L. The lab phoned the result as a critical value. Patient reports mild muscle weakness and palpitations over the past 2 days. Has CKD Stage 4 and recently had his Losartan dose increased from 50mg to 100mg daily. Also started Spironolactone 25mg daily 1 week ago for resistant hypertension. Has been eating large amounts of fruit (oranges, bananas, tomatoes) as part of a \"healthy diet.\"",
    pmh: "CKD Stage 4 (eGFR 22), Hypertension (resistant), Diabetes Type 2. Also has Obesity.",
    medHx: "Losartan 100mg daily (recently increased), Spironolactone 25mg daily (new, started 1 week ago), Amlodipine 10mg, Metformin 500mg BID, Furosemide 40mg BID, Atorvastatin 20mg",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Generalised muscle weakness, areflexic. HR 50 (bradycardic), BP 140/80. ECG: Peaked T waves (tented, narrow-based), absent P waves, widened QRS (120ms). Lethargic but conscious.",
    vitals: "HR: 145 bpm, BP: 84/45 mmHg, RR: 26 breaths/min, Temp: 39°C, SpO2: 87%",
    labs: "K+ 7.0 mEq/L (critical). Na+ 137, Cl- 105, Bicarb 16 (metabolic acidosis). Creatinine 2.8 mg/dL (eGFR 22). BUN 65 mg/dL. Glucose 150 mg/dL. Mg 1.8 mg/dL. Digoxin level: not on digoxin.",
    imaging: "ECG: Peaked T waves (tented), prolonged PR, absent P wave, widened QRS (120ms) — life-threatening hyperkalaemia.",
    diagnosis: "Life-Threatening Hyperkalaemia (K+ 7.0) with ECG Changes — Exacerbated by ACEi + MRA + High-K+ Diet in CKD",
    ddx: ["Hyperkalaemia (drug-induced)", "Hyperkalaemia (CKD)", "Hypokalaemic Periodic Paralysis", "Digoxin Toxicity", "Acute Kidney Injury"],
    goals: "Immediately lower serum potassium to <6.0, protect the heart, correct acidosis, identify and remove precipitating factors",
    pharm: "1. Cardiac protection (IMMEDIATE): IV Calcium Gluconate 10% 10-20mL (or Calcium Chloride 5-10mL) over 2-5 minutes. Repeat in 5 minutes if ECG changes persist. 2. Shift K+ intracellularly (temporary): Nebulised Salbutamol 10-20mg (onset 30 min, reduces K+ by 0.5-1.5). OR IV Insulin 10 units + 50% Dextrose 50mL (onset 15 min, peak 30-60 min). 3. Remove K+ from body: IV Furosemide 40-80mg (if some renal function). KCl-binding resin: Calcium Resonium 15g TID PO/PR. Consider haemodialysis (definitive, if K+ remains >6.5 despite medical therapy). 4. Reverse precipitating factors: STOP Spironolactone. STOP/Hold Losartan (ACEi/ARB). Stop K+-sparing drugs. Review diet. 5. Correct acidosis: IV Sodium Bicarbonate 50-100 mEq (if pH <7.2).",
    nonPharm: "Cardiac monitoring continuously. Strict I/O. Limit K+ intake (avoid high-K+ foods). Identify and stop offending medications.",
    carePlan: "EMERGENCY: IV Calcium Gluconate immediately for ECG changes. Then insulin+glucose, salbutamol, furosemide. Check K+ q1h. Consider dialysis if K+ refractory. Admit to HDU/ICU. Review all medications for K+ elevation risk.",
    dtps: "1. Acute life-threatening hyperkalaemia from combined use of ACEi + MRA + high-K+ diet in advanced CKD. 2. Polypharmacy-related adverse event requiring urgent medical management.",
    monitoring: "Continuous ECG monitoring. K+ q1h until <6.0, then q4-6h. Glucose q1h (if insulin given). Creatinine and bicarbonate. Monitor for hypoglycaemia after insulin/dextrose (recheck glucose q30min x 2h).",
    counselling: "This is a serious electrolyte disturbance. The medications you take for your kidneys and blood pressure can raise potassium, especially when combined with a high-potassium diet. We need to adjust your medications and diet. Avoid: oranges, bananas, tomatoes, potatoes, avocado, and salt substitutes.",
    followUp: "Nephrology review for medication reconciliation. Spironolactone should not be restarted. Dose-reduce Losartan. Dietary counselling. Check K+ 1 week after medication changes.",
    pearls: "Hyperkalaemia is a medical emergency — ECG changes indicate imminent cardiac arrest. Calcium Gluconate does NOT lower K+ — it stabilises the cardiac membrane. The shifters (insulin+glucose, salbutamol, bicarbonate) are temporary measures that last 2-4 hours. The removers (Furosemide, Resonium, dialysis) are definitive. Insulin+glucose is the most predictable and effective shifter. The triad of ACEi/ARB + MRA + CKD is the most common cause of life-threatening hyperkalaemia.",
    references: ["KDIGO Guidelines: CKD-MBD and Electrolytes", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Electrolyte Disorders", "UK Renal Association: Hyperkalaemia"],
